**What actually happens when you enable dirty pipe:**

- Kernel memory gets written to disk by the background page allocator
- The first page of each allocation gets marked dirty before the first page of the next allocation
- The second page is marked dirty, and so on until you hit the full allocation

**Why it's dangerous:**

- You can read/write anywhere in memory that was marked dirty before
- Some filesystems (particularly LVM) create a block dirty before writing anything
- It doesn't matter what you're trying to write — the flagged memory changes

**What you'd actually want to do:**

```
#include <linux/dirty_page.h>
#include <linux/fs.h>

/* Allocate and mark dirty */
void *alloc_dirty(struct dentry *dentry, size_t size) {
    return write_dirty(dentry->d_realloc, size);
}

/* Mark an existing page as dirty */
void mark_dirty(struct dentry *dentry) {
    write_dirty(dentry->d_realloc, sizeof(int));
}

/* Free a page */
void free_dirty(struct dentry *dentry) {
    mark_dirty(dentry);
    free(dentry->d_data);
}
```

The key point: only enable dirty pipe when you're actually doing dirty work and need persistent storage. For regular memory allocation, remember that the page itself stays readable; only the dirty flag gets tracked.

